WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.020
Sometimes, it’s the details of a film that
matter most.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:28.740
Like… a single shot… a needle drop…

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.699
— “I see a little silhouette of-“ — a
line of dialogue…

00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:33.210
— “Here’s lookin’ at you, kid.”

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.539
— a sudden cut…

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:40.390
What seem like insignificant choices often
say more in under twenty seconds than other

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:42.179
films say in two hours.

00:00:42.179 --> 00:00:47.079
In this new format, we’ll be studying these
seemingly trivial pieces, revealing how even

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:51.559
the smallest element can bring layers of meaning
to a piece of art.

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:55.239
Welcome to Wisecrack’s THE FILM TOURIST:
The Wolf of Wall Street.

00:00:55.239 --> 00:00:58.489
“Sell me this pen!

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:02.670
Sell me… this pen.”

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.940
The closing shot of The Wolf of Wall Street.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.400
“This pen works…”

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.960
Just a simple push-in and crane-up.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:19.080
Well… what if I told you this shot changes
the meaning of the entire film?

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.860
“This pen works, and I personally love—“

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:28.170
As a client describes ‘the pen’, his voice
drowns out and solemn music kicks in.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:34.080
Up to this point, the film has utilized poppy,
upbeat hits to underscore the debauchery.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:41.180
“The amnesia phase.”

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.890
So, why the sudden shift to something more
serious and meditative?

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:50.650
For most of its three-hour runtime, The Wolf
of Wall Street revels in Jordan Belfort’s

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:57.710
excess: his yacht, his women, his drugs, his
parties -- the lifestyle paid for by defrauding

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:58.710
investors.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:04.170
That is, until the FBI circles in, seizing
Belfort’s cash and sending him away to prison

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.720
for four years.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:10.269
But unlike similar films— where, the corrupt
Wall Street broker gets locked up and justice

00:02:10.269 --> 00:02:14.739
is served — The Wolf of Wall Street is absent
any overt moralizing.

00:02:14.739 --> 00:02:19.200
Belfort’s prison sentence is a cakewalk,
and he doesn’t even serve his full sentence.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.439
In the end, nothing has changed.

00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:26.019
Belfort is still selling and the people, well,
they’re still buying.

00:02:26.019 --> 00:02:27.830
But this is just the tip of the iceberg.

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:33.110
The final shot can be interpreted in a number
of ways, but here are our three favorites.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:38.709
One: the final shot holds up a mirror to us,
the moviegoers viewing the film.

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:43.189
Notice there’s a backlight behind the seminar
that evokes a projector shining on the back

00:02:43.189 --> 00:02:44.290
of an audience.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:45.879
So, what does this mean?

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:51.121
Well, it may to easy to lay the blame solely
on Jordan for all the moral depravity in The

00:02:51.121 --> 00:02:52.189
Wolf of Wall Street.

00:02:52.189 --> 00:02:54.980
“Jordan!” “Jordan, Jordan, listen!”
“Jordan?

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:55.980
Jordan?”

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:56.980
“Jordan!” “Jordan!”

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:57.980
“You!”

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:03.250
But, perhaps the final shot lays the blame
not only on Belfort, but on us, and the culture

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.319
that allows and encourages his behavior.

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:08.279
“My wife might divorce me but, yeah, let’s
do it.”

00:03:08.279 --> 00:03:11.799
Early on, Belfort says, “Every person you’re
on the phone with, they want to get rich and

00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:12.799
they want to get rich quickly.

00:03:12.799 --> 00:03:16.519
They all want something for nothing.”

00:03:16.519 --> 00:03:21.909
We may feel dignified because we’re not
crawling on the ground f**ked up on quaaludes,

00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:26.650
but truth is, Jordan’s scam wouldn’t work
if we didn’t share his dream of getting

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.080
rich quickly.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.060
Wanting something for nothing…

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.450
We all want to be just like him.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:38.419
Scorsese is essentially calling us out - we’ve
just spent three hours partying with Jordan,

00:03:38.419 --> 00:03:40.550
laughing and reveling in his exploits.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:46.159
It’s no wonder Jordan doesn’t suffer any
punishment in the end, because, after all,

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:52.200
we revel in everything he does, living vicariously
through him, wishing that we could live his

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.410
life.

00:03:53.410 --> 00:04:01.620
Two: do all these people staring intently
at one person, hanging on his every word remind

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:02.680
you of something?

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.209
Yeah, we’re gonna go there.

00:04:05.209 --> 00:04:10.609
Scorsese, a devout catholic, has spent much
of his career exploring his faith in films.

00:04:10.609 --> 00:04:17.000
Yet, in The Wolf of Wall Street characters
worship at the altar of something else: MONEY.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:26.140
The camera holds on these people: each one
in focus so we can see their painfully average

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:31.620
faces — desperate, plain-looking people
looking for meaning; desiring that which can

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.220
elevate them above their mundane existence.

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:40.190
Is Scorcese cynically likening the transformative
power of faith to the transformative power

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.020
of money?

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.780
Except The Almighty never promised a million
bucks in eight easy steps.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:51.770
Three: what if by solemnly portraying a crowd
that worships Jordan, despite his detestable

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:55.590
actions, Scorsese suggests something really
sinister?

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:58.500
That we actually like being taken advantage
of.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:04:59.500
“Oh, baby!

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:00.500
You’re gonna play rough, huh?”

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.740
French philosopher Jean-Francois Lyotard famously
said as much —

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:09.820
“The English unemployed did not become workers
to survive, they… enjoyed the hysterical,

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:14.681
masochistic, whatever exhaustion it was of
hanging on the mines, in the foundries, in

00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:18.220
the factories, in hell, they enjoyed it…

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:19.220
— “Ah!

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:22.360
Jesus Christ!”— … enjoyed the mad destruction
of their organic bodies…

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:23.360
— “I like it!

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:27.420
I like it!” — the decomposition of their
personal identity, —&nbsp;“You are lower than

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.720
pond scum.” — … enjoyed the dissolution
of their families and villages, and enjoyed

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:36.770
the new monstrous anonymity of the suburbs
and the pubs in the morning and evening.”

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:41.900
So, could this closing image indicate the
voluntary submission of the masses, who not

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:47.330
only don’t mind white collar criminals stealing
their money, but find a perverse enjoyment

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:51.830
in their banal existence under the heel of
the Jordan Belforts of the world?

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:56.620
This one single shot is emblematic of just
how complex The Wolf of Wall Street is.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:02.100
As a true totem of our culture of excess,
the film defies easy answers.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:27.520
This format is a bit of a test for us, so,
let us know

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:41.780
what

00:06:41.780 --> 00:07:39.700
you think in

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.030
the comments.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:51.320
And as always, guys, thanks for watching.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:55.760
Peace.

